1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,480
We're going to describe a class of tests called permutation tests, and in particular two-sample

2
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:25,620
permutation tests. Now permutation tests are a family of tests which are at least when sample

3
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:38,280
sizes are not small, they're computationally intensive. Luckily today we have software

4
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:51,660
available and the software package R provides commands for performing permutation tests.

5
00:00:51,660 --> 00:01:04,900
Well, we'll illustrate with an example. An example is a US trial, a civil trial, a discrimination

6
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:18,900
case from 1981, and the plaintiff was named Andrea Capacci, and she brought a case to the

7
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:31,900
court against her employers, Katz and Besthoff, Inc. Now you can Google this trial if you

8
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:40,900
like. And this trial was a sexual discrimination trial, and as part of the evidence there were

9
00:01:40,900 --> 00:02:01,900
data presented about times to promotion in months for men and women in the company over a period

10
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:13,900
of time. So, for the men's times to promotion, the figures in order were something like this,

11
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:27,900
5, 7, 12, 40, 40, won't give the full data here. There were 22 promotion times for men, and the

12
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:39,900
sample size was 483. So let's say N1 sample size for the men was 22. There were only actually two women

13
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:54,900
who got promoted, and their promotion times were 229 and 453. So, what would our null hypothesis be here? Well, we'll

14
00:02:54,900 --> 00:03:03,900
return to the notion of the extent of inference in general, just at the end of this little lecture. We

15
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:10,900
noticed that really the data here are not samples, they represent the complete data that were available

16
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:18,900
for individuals who were promoted over a particular time period. The time period itself is a form of a sample, but

17
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:27,900
given the time period was determined by the duration of time that the plaintiff Kapachi had been in the

18
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:36,900
employment of the company, that was kind of fixed. So, the process by which the data give rise to the

19
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:44,900
the the process by which these promotion times are created, I suppose, is the the essence of what we're

20
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:55,900
interested in. And whatever the processes were, they give rise to some data, we've observed the data. The

21
00:03:55,900 --> 00:04:20,900
null hypothesis would be that the distribution of times to promotion for men and women were the same over the

22
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:30,900
time period in question. This would, of course, imply that they have the same means, the same medians.

23
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:43,900
What would we take as the alternative hypothesis? Well, for sure, the distributions are different, which would

24
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:48,900
simply be the negation of the null hypothesis. But I would argue here that one should consider only a

25
00:04:48,900 --> 00:05:08,900
one-sided alternative. The one-sided alternative being that the women's promotion times are greater, on average. So, the

26
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:15,900
distribution of the distribution of the women's promotion times was, on average, larger. So, I had a greater

27
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:29,900
mean, say, or greater median than that for the men. Now, why would I take a one-sided case here? I would argue that, given

28
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:39,900
that this is a discrimination trial, it is inconceivable that a case would make it to the court for a claim for

29
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:46,900
discrimination against women, gender discrimination, where in fact there was evidence that there was positive

30
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:56,900
discrimination, or it was plausible, in any way, that there was positive discrimination towards women. Hence, I consider here the

31
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:04,900
only logical alternative one can take to the null is an alternative which is one-sided. And that is the case that

32
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:13,900
we will consider. So, what do we do in a permutation test? Well, we choose some test statistic or other. So, what should I

33
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:23,900
choose as a test statistic? You can pretty much choose anything that would be a sensible choice. I'm going to choose something very

34
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:44,900
simple. The sum of the women's promotion times, which in this case is 229 plus 453 is 682. That might strike you as

35
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:50,900
being an odd choice for test statistic, just the sum of the data for the women. In fact, I'd get exactly the same inference

36
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:58,900
inference here, if I chose the sample means, the difference in the sample means of the test statistic, or even the t

37
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:05,900
statistic. So, if you were doing a two-sample t-test here, well, you've only got two observations for the women's group, but you

38
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:19,900
could do a two-sample t-test, and I'd get the same inference, actually. I think I'm at it saying. So, the question we ask ourselves is, we have data,

39
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:47,900
we have data for men, and we have data for women, and we have 22 observations for the men, and two for the women's, so 24 observations in total, two of which were women's, and 22 were men. So, we ask ourselves, well, how many, I'll say regroupings, are there, of 24 observations, where we put two in one group,

40
00:07:47,900 --> 00:08:09,900
and 22 in another? Well, the answer is, of course, 24 choose 2. So, 24 observations. I don't really care about the ordering in each group, I just care about how many ways I can put two in one group and 22 in another.

41
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:24,500
And if we work that out, that comes to 276. So, there are 276 ways of taking 24 numbers and putting two in one group, call that the women, and 22 in the other.

42
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:28,420
And under the null hypothesis, well, all of those regroupings should be equally likely.

43
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:44,140
Now, the question that we ask is, how many of those give a value of the test statistic at least as inconsistent as the one that we've observed, which is 682?

44
00:08:44,140 --> 00:08:47,420
Well, we can enumerate them.

45
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:54,620
In particular, the one that we observed, so we always include that one.

46
00:08:55,860 --> 00:08:59,480
So, that was the one that we observed. What else might we have observed?

47
00:08:59,480 --> 00:09:04,700
Well, we might have observed 229 and 483.

48
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:15,380
We might have observed 453 and 483, the two largest values.

49
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:34,980
So, those three ways to group our data to give a test statistic

50
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:54,220
at least as inconsistent with the null hypothesis as the one that we observed.

51
00:10:01,220 --> 00:10:02,960
So, how do we find our p-value?

52
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:09,240
Well, our p-value says, well, actually there were 276 ways,

53
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:13,620
all of which should be equally likely of grouping the data,

54
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:20,400
of which three gave a value at least as inconsistent as the one we observed.

55
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:22,120
Of course, that includes the one we observed.

56
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:28,860
And given that we're doing a one-tailed test,

57
00:10:28,860 --> 00:10:33,860
the p-value is just based on that tail,

58
00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:38,360
and we have a p-value of 0.01,

59
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,340
so around 1%.

60
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:44,160
So, that's quite strong evidence

61
00:10:44,160 --> 00:10:56,000
against the null hypothesis that the men and women's promotion times

62
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:57,440
were from the same distribution.

63
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:01,780
Notice that if we did a two-tailed test,

64
00:11:01,780 --> 00:11:06,660
we'd also arrive at a conclusion that there was evidence,

65
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:07,900
although a little less strong,

66
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:10,040
against the null hypothesis.

67
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:13,440
Let's talk in generality now,

68
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:17,280
and describe what we've done in the general case,

69
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:20,540
where we've got two samples

70
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:23,120
of size,

71
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:29,100
let's call them M and N, respectively.

72
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:33,320
So, I choose a test statistic.

73
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:40,300
You can simply choose a test statistic

74
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:45,560
that is a test statistic for a test that you already know,

75
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:48,200
so you could choose a two-sample-t-test statistic,

76
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:50,840
or rank sum, or whatever.

77
00:11:55,320 --> 00:11:57,780
We find, at least in principle,

78
00:11:57,780 --> 00:11:59,580
the test statistic

79
00:11:59,580 --> 00:12:03,120
that we chose

80
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:05,160
for all

81
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:06,240
of the

82
00:12:06,240 --> 00:12:08,100
n plus m

83
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:09,060
to n

84
00:12:09,060 --> 00:12:13,220
regroupings

85
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:15,920
of the data.

86
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:20,060
So, all the ways in which

87
00:12:20,060 --> 00:12:22,320
we can assign the n plus m

88
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:22,900
numbers

89
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:25,800
into a group of n and a group of m.

90
00:12:27,780 --> 00:12:31,540
find the number

91
00:12:31,540 --> 00:12:33,480
of samples

92
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:39,560
that have a test statistic

93
00:12:39,560 --> 00:12:47,360
at least

94
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:49,540
as inconsistent

95
00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:54,740
with the null hypothesis

96
00:12:54,740 --> 00:13:00,300
as not observed.

97
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:02,740
It's the one that we saw.

98
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:06,600
And we divide that number

99
00:13:06,600 --> 00:13:12,300
so that count

100
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:14,880
by

101
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:17,020
n plus m

102
00:13:17,020 --> 00:13:17,800
to n

103
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:22,100
to give our p-value.

104
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:24,500
So, let's just

105
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:25,880
talk a little bit more

106
00:13:25,880 --> 00:13:26,920
about our example

107
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:28,980
in this

108
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:30,160
Apache case.

109
00:13:30,820 --> 00:13:31,040
So,

110
00:13:31,720 --> 00:13:33,000
the data in the trial

111
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,340
was such that

112
00:13:37,340 --> 00:13:38,260
if we choose

113
00:13:38,260 --> 00:13:38,780
t

114
00:13:38,780 --> 00:13:40,200
as our test statistic,

115
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:41,180
let's say it's the

116
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:49,820
total of the women's

117
00:13:49,820 --> 00:13:55,240
promotion times.

118
00:13:56,720 --> 00:13:57,800
We notice that

119
00:13:57,800 --> 00:13:58,480
if we look back

120
00:13:58,480 --> 00:13:59,060
at our data,

121
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:00,680
the smallest value

122
00:14:00,680 --> 00:14:01,420
that could have taken

123
00:14:01,420 --> 00:14:02,620
across the regroupings

124
00:14:02,620 --> 00:14:04,640
is when the two

125
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:05,560
smallest observations

126
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:08,180
were for the women,

127
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:10,180
which were five and seven.

128
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:11,300
So, there's one

129
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:13,780
out of the 276

130
00:14:13,780 --> 00:14:14,620
groupings

131
00:14:15,420 --> 00:14:16,320
that gives a value

132
00:14:16,320 --> 00:14:16,940
of 12

133
00:14:16,940 --> 00:14:17,900
for our test statistic.

134
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:20,180
The next smallest value

135
00:14:20,180 --> 00:14:21,860
is 17.

136
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:23,900
Again, there's only one way

137
00:14:23,900 --> 00:14:24,580
to do that.

138
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:25,740
The next smallest value

139
00:14:25,740 --> 00:14:26,280
is 19

140
00:14:26,280 --> 00:14:27,040
and actually there's

141
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:27,880
quite a few ways

142
00:14:27,880 --> 00:14:29,480
that can arise

143
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:31,560
and so on.

144
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:33,080
So, we get

145
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:35,800
different numbers

146
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:36,440
of ways.

147
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:40,160
So, remember,

148
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:41,020
this is the sampling

149
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:41,980
distribution of this

150
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:42,960
test statistic

151
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:44,900
under the null hypothesis

152
00:14:44,900 --> 00:14:47,980
and we actually observed

153
00:14:47,980 --> 00:14:49,720
something way up

154
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:50,440
in the upper tail,

155
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:52,040
682,

156
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:56,380
and we were doing

157
00:14:56,380 --> 00:14:57,600
a one tail test

158
00:14:57,600 --> 00:15:00,320
and those three

159
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:01,200
cases together

160
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:02,200
would only have

161
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:02,780
probability

162
00:15:02,780 --> 00:15:05,700
of about 0.1

163
00:15:05,700 --> 00:15:08,060
if all of these

164
00:15:08,060 --> 00:15:08,740
possibilities,

165
00:15:09,320 --> 00:15:11,200
all of the 276

166
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:12,100
were equally likely.

167
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:14,280
So, that seems large,

168
00:15:14,340 --> 00:15:14,900
it seems to be

169
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:16,040
way up in the upper tail

170
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:17,260
if, in fact,

171
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:20,140
T really was

172
00:15:20,140 --> 00:15:20,740
following this

173
00:15:20,740 --> 00:15:21,440
distribution

174
00:15:21,440 --> 00:15:25,220
under our null hypothesis.

175
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:26,200
So, as always,

176
00:15:26,280 --> 00:15:26,960
when we're performing

177
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:27,580
a test,

178
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:29,340
we're considering

179
00:15:29,340 --> 00:15:30,420
how does that

180
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:31,580
test statistic behave

181
00:15:31,580 --> 00:15:32,640
if the null hypothesis

182
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:33,340
was true?

183
00:15:34,060 --> 00:15:34,660
And here,

184
00:15:34,760 --> 00:15:35,420
we're doing a brute

185
00:15:35,420 --> 00:15:36,200
force approach.

186
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:37,240
We're actually

187
00:15:37,240 --> 00:15:39,300
computing this

188
00:15:39,300 --> 00:15:40,640
sampling distribution

189
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:41,240
explicitly.

190
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:42,040
We're really only

191
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:43,000
interested in the tails,

192
00:15:43,140 --> 00:15:43,840
but we should have

193
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:44,960
this picture in our mind.

194
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:48,860
Interestingly enough,

195
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:50,800
the Capacci case

196
00:15:50,800 --> 00:15:53,240
was defeated

197
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:57,020
even, I understand,

198
00:15:57,360 --> 00:15:58,400
after an appeal.

199
00:16:00,220 --> 00:16:00,800
Of course,

200
00:16:01,040 --> 00:16:02,140
although these data

201
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:02,960
look compelling,

202
00:16:03,600 --> 00:16:04,600
there's more to the data

203
00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:06,400
than meets the eye.

204
00:16:06,520 --> 00:16:07,240
There's always a story

205
00:16:07,240 --> 00:16:08,160
behind our data.

206
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:10,380
And, of course,

207
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:11,560
the data

208
00:16:11,560 --> 00:16:13,380
only included information

209
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:14,020
for people

210
00:16:14,020 --> 00:16:15,080
who made it to promotion.

211
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:16,380
So, there's no

212
00:16:16,380 --> 00:16:17,440
information about

213
00:16:17,440 --> 00:16:18,280
people who

214
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:20,500
served the company

215
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:21,440
and who didn't get

216
00:16:21,440 --> 00:16:22,420
promoted in that time.

217
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:23,540
And there was also

218
00:16:23,540 --> 00:16:24,320
evidence that there

219
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:25,340
were women who had

220
00:16:25,340 --> 00:16:27,400
turned down promotion,

221
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:28,260
so had been offered

222
00:16:28,260 --> 00:16:29,740
promotion but refused it.

223
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:31,440
I think on the grounds

224
00:16:31,440 --> 00:16:32,340
that the nature of the

225
00:16:32,340 --> 00:16:33,320
work changed once they

226
00:16:33,320 --> 00:16:33,920
got promoted

227
00:16:33,920 --> 00:16:35,600
and didn't suit

228
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:36,000
some women.

229
00:16:36,840 --> 00:16:38,280
So, although the data

230
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:39,040
looked quite,

231
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:40,300
these data looked

232
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:42,140
quite convincing,

233
00:16:42,140 --> 00:16:44,240
the case was

234
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:46,500
defeated in the event.

235
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:50,120
Now, let's just

236
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:51,060
conclude here

237
00:16:51,060 --> 00:16:52,520
with a brief

238
00:16:52,520 --> 00:16:53,480
talk

239
00:16:53,480 --> 00:16:54,920
about

240
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:56,500
the extent

241
00:16:56,500 --> 00:16:58,160
of statistical

242
00:16:58,160 --> 00:16:58,700
inference.

243
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:00,880
Because, as we

244
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:01,480
described

245
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:02,680
in this example,

246
00:17:03,300 --> 00:17:04,220
the data were not

247
00:17:04,220 --> 00:17:04,820
a sample.

248
00:17:05,300 --> 00:17:05,720
So, normally,

249
00:17:05,820 --> 00:17:06,280
when we're doing

250
00:17:06,280 --> 00:17:06,760
statistics,

251
00:17:06,980 --> 00:17:07,500
we're thinking to

252
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:08,360
ourselves that we

253
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:09,400
have some sort

254
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:10,760
of population,

255
00:17:10,760 --> 00:17:12,380
so some sort

256
00:17:12,380 --> 00:17:12,840
of set

257
00:17:12,840 --> 00:17:14,740
of individuals

258
00:17:14,740 --> 00:17:16,360
or objects

259
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:17,380
or something,

260
00:17:18,540 --> 00:17:20,240
and we take

261
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:22,000
a sample

262
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:23,500
from that

263
00:17:23,500 --> 00:17:25,100
population.

264
00:17:26,180 --> 00:17:27,220
We hope,

265
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:30,000
or it should be

266
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:30,320
hoped,

267
00:17:30,700 --> 00:17:31,600
that we take

268
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:32,100
the sample

269
00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:33,360
in some sensible

270
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:34,780
way as to

271
00:17:34,780 --> 00:17:35,820
minimise bias.

272
00:17:36,280 --> 00:17:37,460
And a simple

273
